N('leavenworth-downtown-historic-district-ks','Leavenworth Downtown Historic District','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',39.3179,-94.915,'site','public','walk','','1854–',
  'Leavenworth\'s 45-block downtown was planned in 1854 and in its first two decades commerce boomed. Most of the early stores were built as blocks of several storefronts with shared upper floors. The city says many of these buildings are gone, or survive only as lower floors after fire, tornado and neglect, and that car parking replaced buildings after World War II. The district was listed in the National Register in 2002.',
  'Block-style commercial buildings in the streets round Delaware, Cherokee, Fifth and Shawnee. The city names the O\'Donnell Block at 100 S. 5th Street, the Masonic Temple at 421 Delaware, the Yum block at 311 to 321 S. 5th Street and an intact row of stores in the 600 block of Cherokee Street. It counts 65 contributing buildings, mostly red brick with cast iron and terra cotta trim, in Colonial and Classical Revival variations; two of them, at 429 and 500 Delaware, were listed in the Register on their own earlier. At 330 Delaware (1910) the city says an original third storey was removed after tornado damage, and the Knights of Columbus Block at 331 Delaware (about 1882) was remodelled about 1945. Interiors not checked: the shops and offices are private and keep their own hours.',
  'A Missouri River town\'s 1850s to 1900s commercial blocks, brick and cast iron.',
  'Public city streets and sidewalks (the street is the city\'s; the owners of the individual buildings were not checked); the buildings are private shops and offices with their own hours, which were not checked. Parking was not checked: look for street parking round Delaware and Fifth.',
  [['City of Leavenworth: Downtown Historic District',LVC+'downtown-historic-district'],['Kansas Historical Society: Leavenworth County register listings',KSR%(3,'LV')]],brick='yes',apart='axa-building-leavenworth-ks: one building on South Fifth Street; this entry is the whole downtown district'),
N('south-esplanade-district-leavenworth-ks','South Esplanade Historic District, Leavenworth','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',39.3105,-94.90757,'site','public','walk','','1857–',
  'The South Esplanade is a street in an 1857 addition to Leavenworth. On its east side the ground falls away to an unobstructed view of the first truly eastward bend in the Missouri River. The city says it was first named Main Street and was joined to North Esplanade by a bridge over Three Mile Creek that still exists but can no longer carry traffic. The 1993 flood caused much of the hillside to collapse, and the slope was cleared and a terrace built partway down. The district was listed in the National Register in 2002.',
  'A street of houses along the bluff, from the late 1800s into the 1900s. The city\'s walking tour names the 1895 Stick Style house at 800 S. Second Street, built for shoe store owner William Drechsel, with three small cottages built for coal miners beside it; the Queen Anne house at 814 South Esplanade, with stained glass round the front window; and a 1924 mail-order kit house at 100 Olive from the Gordon-Van Tyne Company of Iowa. From the bluff the dry dock of the old Missouri Valley shipyard can be seen; the yard built landing craft in World War II and launched its last tugboat in June 1983. Interiors not checked: the houses are private homes.',
  'Bluff-top houses with a view of the Missouri River bend and the shipyard dry dock.',
  'A public street. The houses are private homes; look from the sidewalk. Parking was not checked; the city\'s tour begins at 2nd and Olive.',
  [['City of Leavenworth: South Esplanade Historic District',LVC+'south-esplanade-historic-district'],['Kansas Historical Society: Leavenworth County register listings',KSR%(5,'LV')]],brick='?'),
N('albert-doege-house-leavenworth-ks','Albert Doege House','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',39.31269,-94.90854,'exact','view','walk','','1864',
  'An 1864 house at 108 Olive Street that the city\'s tour calls significant for its age and its mix of Federal and Italianate features. Albert Doege owned the National Soap Company, which made laundry and toilet soap, violet starch, sal soda and bluing. The city says Jeanette and Manny Craig saved the house from demolition. It is on the Kansas state register; it stands just outside the South Esplanade district.',
  'An 1864 house with Federal and Italianate details. Interior not checked.',
  'An 1864 house with Federal and Italianate details, saved from demolition.',
  'A private house as far as the city\'s page shows; who owns it now and whether it is open were not checked. Look from the public sidewalk.',
  [['City of Leavenworth: South Esplanade Historic District (the tour entry)',LVC+'south-esplanade-historic-district'],['Kansas Historical Society: Leavenworth County register listings',KSR%(1,'LV')]],brick='?'),
N('north-broadway-district-leavenworth-ks','North Broadway Historic District and St. Joseph Catholic Church, Leavenworth','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',39.32028,-94.9214,'site','view','walk','','1858–',
  'The district lies in the city\'s 1858 Western Addition. Developer Elijah Hughes meant North Broadway to be lined with mansions, with smaller houses on the cross streets (Miami, Osage and Ottawa). By 1865, when most of the grand houses went up, Leavenworth had five brickyards, a foundry, quarries and plenty of lumber. The district is anchored by St. Joseph Catholic Church, the German parish, built to a design by a Carmelite priest, Cyril Knoll, who received the parish in 1864. The city counts 39 contributing buildings on about seven acres.',
  'St. Joseph Catholic Church (1868, 1891) is a Gothic Revival brick church in random-course American bond with pink mortar joints; its tower and corner piers have pyramidal caps and a band of blind lancet arches under the cornice. It is joined to the priory, and the grounds hold a sandstone grotto with a Madonna and Child statue of about 1940 and a county historical society plaque. The street also has Italianate, Queen Anne and Romanesque houses; the city calls one two-storey Italianate house the oldest building in the district. Interiors not checked, and the church\'s service times were not checked.',
  'Leavenworth\'s mansion street, with the German parish\'s Gothic brick church.',
  'Public streets; the houses are private homes and the church keeps its own hours, not checked. Look from the sidewalk. Parking was not checked.',
  [['City of Leavenworth: North Broadway Historic District',LVC+'north-broadway-historic-district']],brick='yes'),
N('union-park-district-leavenworth-ks','Union Park Historic District, Leavenworth','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',39.31067,-94.91705,'site','public','walk','','1857–',
  'A triangular park of about six acres just south of downtown, in two subdivisions platted in 1857. In December 1870 local citizens dedicated it "for a Public Park for the use of the Public forever". A fountain, fence and trees were added in the 1880s; the park then decayed until a volunteer effort in 1997 brought it back, with a replica fountain and period-style lamps, and it was rededicated in 1999. The district was listed in the National Register in 2002.',
  'The park, with its replica fountain, and eleven buildings round it: houses in Italianate, Second Empire, Queen Anne, Romanesque and other styles, carriage houses, and the old Hose House No. 2 at 903 Fifth Avenue (the city\'s first hook and ladder truck from 1905; the fire department left in 1973). Next to it, the Fields Building at 901 Fifth (about 1880) was restored from a photograph and won a state preservation award in 1999. Interiors not checked: the buildings are private.',
  'A small Victorian park ringed by houses and an old horse-drawn fire station.',
  'The park was dedicated in 1870 for public use, as the city\'s page says; the present owner was not checked beyond that. The buildings are private; look from the sidewalk. Parking was not checked.',
  [['City of Leavenworth: Union Park Historic District',LVC+'union-park-historic-district'],['Kansas Historical Society: Leavenworth County register listings',KSR%(5,'LV')]],brick='?'),
N('white-feather-spring-kck','White Feather Spring, Argentine, Kansas City, Kansas','Kansas City, Kansas','ks','curio','marker',39.07144,-94.67344,'area','view','drive','','1826–1836',
  'A natural spring in the Argentine district, named for Susan White Feather, the first owner of the land after the 1854 treaty division. Wikipedia says Tenskwatawa, the Shawnee Prophet and younger brother of Tecumseh, set up a village in 1826 near what is now South 26th Street and Woodend Avenue, later moved to the spring, and died there in November 1836. In 1897 Charles Bluejacket, who had been at the burial, pointed out the spring and the grave site; a temporary marker was put up and later removed, and the exact grave is not known. The spring was listed in the National Register in 1975.',
  'A spring and a marker. Wikipedia says the spring is on private property, and a photograph on Commons shows a marker for the Shawnee Prophet near it; where the marker stands and whether it can be read from a public street were not checked.',
  'Where the Shawnee Prophet lived and died; the grave site is unmarked and unknown.',
  'Private property, according to Wikipedia. Do not go onto the land; look from the street at 3818 Ruby Avenue only if the marker can be seen from there, which was not checked.',
  [W('White Feather Spring'),['Kansas Historical Society: Wyandotte County register listings',KSR%(5,'WY')]],brick='no',conf='mixed'),
N('hanover-heights-district-kck','Hanover Heights Historic District, Kansas City, Kansas','Kansas City, Kansas','ks','town','standing',39.0525,-94.61073,'site','view','drive','','1912–1930',
  'A 15.5-acre neighbourhood developed between 1912 and 1930, with 90 contributing buildings between State Line Road and Rainbow Boulevard and between Olathe Boulevard and West 43rd Avenue. It went on the Register of Historic Kansas Places on December 2, 1989 and the National Register on May 17, 1990. The Register lists its styles as Prairie School, Bungalow and Craftsman, and Foursquare. In 2012 it became the centre of the Kansas City Startup Village, which grew round the arrival of Google Fiber.',
  'Houses and apartment buildings of the 1910s and 1920s. Wikipedia names the Boulevard Apartments and the Judge Louis R. Gates Residence (about 1922 to 1923, a Prairie style house at 4146 Cambridge Street, designed by Clarence Shepard, listed separately in 1980). Interiors not checked.',
  'A Prairie School and bungalow neighbourhood of the 1910s and 1920s.',
  'Public streets; the houses are private homes. Look from the sidewalk. Parking was not checked.',
  [W('Hanover Heights Neighborhood Historic District'),['Kansas Historical Society: Wyandotte County register listings',KSR%(2,'WY')]],brick='?'),
